  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 AM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSurface high pressure to our north and northwest will continue to \nextend into our area through Friday as it slides slowly east. The \npost frontal airmass over our area will provide a mild and sunny day \nThursday, with temperatures generally a bit below normal and a \nnoticeable absence of the humid stickiness that was around earlier \nin the week. Friday will be mostly sunny and dry as well, with high \ntemperatures closer to seasonal normals.  \n\nFor the weekend, southerly flow brings an increase in moisture as an \nupper level shortwave energy and trough drop from out of the \nnorthern/central Great Plains into our area. The increase in \nmoisture combined with the incoming trough/low pressure and a \nfrontal boundary will lead to chances for showers and storms \nreturning to the area Saturday into Sunday. The details are still \nnot clear, but while the LREF ensemble data currently suggests the \njoint probability of at least 500 J/kg of CAPE and 25 kts of bulk \nshear are very low (around 10%), there is certainly potential in \nthis pattern for a period favorable for some strong to severe storms \nsince at least marginal shear will likely be present, with the most \nlikely time frame for any severe threat being Saturday into Saturday \nnight. However, whether or not this threat materializes will depend \non details, including the timing of best forcing and available CAPE, \nthat are still unknown. Localized flooding will also be possible in \nany locations that see repeated or prolonged periods of heavy rain \nduring the weekend. This will bear close watching as we get closer. \n\nThere are model differences for the early part of next week with \nsome models showing the upper trough cutting off somewhere over our \nregion and gradually weakening.  If it does, then chances for \nshowers and storms will remain elevated into next week as this upper \nlow continues to slowly spins across the region with no where to go \nbefore it eventually dissipates.",
